      "keys": [
        "A minor",
        "C minor",
        "D minor"
      ],
      "description": "Minimal techno is subtraction as art\\u2014taking the elements of techno and asking how few are truly needed. Pioneered by Richie Hawtin, Ricardo Villalobos, and the Kompakt label in the late 90s, it replaced big builds with micro-variations, bombast with hypnosis. The kick is often understated, sometimes barely there\\u2014more click than thump. The tempo drops to 125 range, slower than Berlin, allowing each element room to breathe and shift. Tracks evolve over 8-10 minutes through tiny, almost imperceptible changes.",
      "production": "Percussion is textural rather than rhythmic\\u2014clicks, blips, filtered noise, organic sounds processed beyond recognition. The hi-hat might be a sliver of white noise, the snare a filtered snap. Elements drift in and out, appearing for 32 bars then vanishing. The bass is often just a sub presence, felt more than heard, pulsing on and off-beats to create movement. When melodic elements appear, they're fragments\\u2014a single note repeating with slight filter modulation, a chord appearing once every 16 bars. The mix is pristine and wide, every element placed precisely in the stereo field. This is headphone music as much as club music\\u2014details reveal themselves on close listening.",
