      "description": "Breakbeat is the broad church of broken rhythm\\u2014any electronic music that doesn't hit four-on-the-floor. The term covers everything from big beat to breaks to nu-skool, but the unifying element is that syncopated, hip-hop-derived kick pattern. The Chemical Brothers and Fatboy Slim brought this to stadiums in the 90s, but the roots go back to electro and hip-hop. The kick lands on the one and off-beats, creating space and bounce that four-on-the-floor can't touch.",
      "production": "The break pattern is usually based on funk breaks\\u2014James Brown's drummers, the Winstons, etc.\\u2014programmed or sampled and processed. The kick sits heavy on one, often with a second hit around beat 3 or 3.5 creating the signature bounce. Snares crack on two and four with attitude. The energy comes from the interplay between kick and snare rather than the steady pulse of house or techno. Bass lines have room to move and groove, often with funkier patterns than four-on-the-floor allows. Synths can be massive\\u2014big acid lines, distorted leads, filtered stabs. The aesthetic is more rock than house, more attitude than subtlety. This is music for fist-pumping.",
