      "description": "Microhouse is the sound of house music put under a microscope \\u2014 every click, cut, and micro-sample magnified into a compositional element. Born in the late 1990s and early 2000s at the intersection of minimal techno, glitch, and deep house, microhouse replaced conventional drum machines and synthesizers with the detritus of digital audio: tiny fragments of sound (sometimes just 10-50 milliseconds long), vinyl crackle, digital artifacts, mouth noises, radio static, and chopped-up fragments of found audio, all assembled into surprisingly groovy, danceable patterns. The genre emerged from two overlapping scenes: the Cologne/D\\u00fcsseldorf axis (Kompakt Records, founded by Wolfgang Voigt and Michael Mayer; Traum Schallplatten) and Berlin (Perlon Records, founded by Zip and Markus Nikolai; Playhouse Records). Kompakt gave us the pop-leaning, melodic side of microhouse \\u2014 warm, shuffling, deceptively pretty music with tiny clicking percussion and lush background pads (the 'Pop Ambient' compilations are the ambient cousin). Perlon gave us the weirder, more abstract, more DJ-tool-oriented side \\u2014 stripped-back groove constructions designed for long, hypnotic sets where the micro-details become the event. The genre's patron saint is Akufen (Marc Leclair from Montreal), whose 2002 album 'My Way' on Force Inc. is the definitive microhouse statement \\u2014 every sound in every track is a micro-sample, a fragment of AM/FM radio captured in real-time, chopped to millisecond lengths, and arranged into house grooves. The hi-hats are snippets of radio static. The kick drum is a chopped vocal consonant. The 'melody' is a cascade of 30ms vocal fragments that individually mean nothing but collectively create rhythm and pitch. It's the logical extreme of Todd Edwards' micro-sampling technique applied to house music's grid. Other essential figures: Ricardo Villalobos (the genre's greatest DJ and one of its key producers \\u2014 his tracks like 'Easy Lee' and 'Dexter' are masterclasses in minimal groove construction from almost nothing, and his DJ sets at Fabric and Panorama Bar defined how microhouse functions on a dancefloor \\u2014 8-hour journeys where a single groove evolves glacially), Richie Hawtin (whose work as Plastikman and on his M_nus label brought glitch aesthetics to the dancefloor), Isol\\u00e9e (whose 'Beau Mot Plage' is arguably microhouse's biggest crossover hit \\u2014 that sparkling, cascading melody over a clicking beat), Luomo/Vladislav Delay (who merged dub techno processing with microhouse grooves and added soulful vocal fragments), Jan Jelinek (whose 'Loop-Finding-Jazz-Records' pushed microhouse into abstract, almost academic territory, building entire pieces from microscopic loops of old jazz vinyl), Thomas Brinkmann (locked grooves on vinyl as source material), Pantha du Prince (whose 'Black Noise' album brought bell tones and organic textures to microhouse), and Sascha Funke, Superpitcher, and the Kompakt roster. The genre peaked commercially around 2002-2006 but its aesthetic DNA permeates all minimal electronic music made since. Every producer who uses granular synthesis, glitch editing, or micro-sampled percussion in a dance context is working in microhouse's shadow.",
      "production": "Microhouse production is built on a radical premise: ANY sound, no matter how small or seemingly unmusical, can become a rhythmic or melodic element if placed precisely on a grid. The core technique is micro-sampling \\u2014 capturing extremely short fragments of audio (5-100ms) from diverse sources: radio broadcasts, field recordings, vinyl surface noise, mouth clicks, breath sounds, digital artifacts, even the sounds of the recording software itself (buffer glitches, plugin artifacts). These fragments are then loaded into a sampler and triggered rhythmically, with pitch, envelope, and filter applied per-hit to create tonal and timbral variation from a single source. Akufen's technique was to record AM/FM radio in real-time, chop it into hundreds of tiny fragments, sort them roughly by timbre (percussive clicks, tonal fragments, noise bursts, vocal snippets), and then sequence them into 4/4 house patterns on a step sequencer. The kick might be a chopped 'p' or 'b' consonant from a radio voice, pitched down and layered with a sine sub. The hi-hat might be a snippet of radio static or tape hiss. The 'melody' emerges from the pitch content of the micro-samples themselves \\u2014 even a 30ms fragment of a singing voice contains pitch information, and when you sequence dozens of these at different pitches, recognizable melodic shapes appear from the chaos. The rhythmic foundation maintains house music's 4/4 kick pattern but the percussion is built entirely from these non-traditional sources. Clicks (short, transient-heavy sounds from digital editing artifacts, vinyl pops, or synthesized with very short noise bursts through tight envelopes \\u2014 attack 0ms, decay 5-20ms) replace hi-hats. Cuts (abrupt sample truncations that create their own percussive character) provide rhythmic accents. The groove comes from extremely precise placement and subtle timing offsets \\u2014 microhouse is quantized to the grid but with tiny human-feel adjustments (\\u00b15-15ms) that give the clicking patterns a shuffling, organic quality despite their digital origins. Bass is typically clean and deep \\u2014 a sine or triangle sub-bass, or a simple filtered analog-style bass, providing the warm harmonic foundation that the cold, clinical micro-samples float above. The contrast between warm bass and clinical clicks is essential to the aesthetic. Melodic content is minimal and often emerges incidentally from the timbral qualities of the samples rather than from deliberate melodic writing. When deliberate melodies appear (as in Isol\\u00e9e's work), they tend to be simple, repetitive phrases with heavy filtering and delay processing \\u2014 bright, sparkling, and slightly melancholic. The arrangement philosophy is SUBTRACTION, not addition. Start with a full groove and slowly remove or replace elements over 8-12 minutes. Changes happen at the micro level \\u2014 a single click in a 16-step pattern is swapped for a different click, the filter on one element opens slightly, a new micro-sample appears in a previously empty step. The listener is trained to focus on tiny details because that's where all the action is. Kompakt-school microhouse tends to be warmer and more melodic, with lush pad layers and recognizable chord progressions underneath the clicking percussion. Perlon-school microhouse is more austere \\u2014 just the clicks, a kick, a bass note, and space. Both approaches share the fundamental commitment to minimal material generating maximum groove.",
